SFML DRM: fall back to XRGB8888 when the framebuffer of an ARGB8888 buffer is refused

SFML makes the GBM surface in ARGB8888 and registers each buffer it renders
with drmModeAddFB2. On an Intel Ironlake (the HD Graphics of the Core i3 5xx
and i5 6xx, Gen5) that call fails with "Invalid argument" for the X-tiled
buffer Mesa gives: SFML prints "Failed to create fb" and "Failed to get FB
from buffer object" for every frame and the screen stays black. The same
buffer in XRGB8888 is accepted (tested with gbm_bo_create + drmModeAddFB2 at
1280x480, 640x480 and 1280x240).

When both attempts fail for an ARGB8888 buffer, the framebuffer is made again
as XRGB8888: the same memory, with the alpha ignored, which is all a display
does with it. A driver that takes ARGB8888 (amdgpu, radeon) never reaches the
new code.

diff --git a/extlibs/SFML/src/SFML/Window/DRM/DRMContext.cpp b/extlibs/SFML/src/SFML/Window/DRM/DRMContext.cpp
--- a/extlibs/SFML/src/SFML/Window/DRM/DRMContext.cpp
+++ b/extlibs/SFML/src/SFML/Window/DRM/DRMContext.cpp
@@ -196,6 +196,24 @@
         result = drmModeAddFB2(drmFd, width, height, format, handles.data(), strides.data(), offsets.data(), &fb->fbId, 0);
     }
 
+    // The i915 on Ironlake (Gen5) does not scan out a format with alpha:
+    // the X-tiled ARGB8888 buffer EGL gives is refused with EINVAL and the screen
+    // stays black. XRGB8888 is the same memory with the alpha ignored, which is
+    // all the display does with it. It only runs after the attempts above fail,
+    // so a driver that takes ARGB8888 is not touched.
+    if (result && format == GBM_FORMAT_ARGB8888)
+    {
+        result = drmModeAddFB2(drmFd,
+                               width,
+                               height,
+                               GBM_FORMAT_XRGB8888,
+                               handles.data(),
+                               strides.data(),
+                               offsets.data(),
+                               &fb->fbId,
+                               0);
+    }
+
     if (result)
     {
         sf::err() << "Failed to create fb: " << std::strerror(errno) << std::endl;
